{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are generally below realized prices by ~1–3 EUR for most of the horizon, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (additive bias correction) when recent trend is positive.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after an upswing begins, the model increases too slowly and stays too low for ~10–20 days. Use a trend-sensitive adjustment: if last 5–10 days momentum is positive, add an extra uplift that decays with horizon.",
    "Late-horizon instability/overshoot: at longer horizons (~day 20–30) the model sometimes drifts too high (e.g., projecting 70+ when outcomes flatten or reverse). Add stronger mean-reversion damping beyond ~2–3 weeks (shrink forecasts toward recent 10–20d average).",
    "Poor handling of turning points: model misses reversals—keeps projecting continuation (either too low during rallies or too high into pullbacks). Introduce a regime switch: if price is extended vs 20d mean (e.g., >+1.5–2 std), cap continuation and pull forecast back toward mean.",
    "Spike response asymmetry: during sharp upward moves, underpredicts the amplitude (needs uplift); after peaks, tends to overpredict persistence (needs faster decay). Apply asymmetric correction: boost near-term forecasts on upside breakouts, but increase decay/mean reversion immediately after local highs."
  ]
}
